class Solution {
  public int dispatchOrderFirstQualifyingSlip(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]degree=new int[n];for(int[]row:a)for(int v=0;v<n;v++)degree[v]+=row[v];Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(degree[v]==0)queue.add(v);while(!queue.isEmpty()){int u=queue.remove();out.add(u);for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0&&--degree[v]==0)queue.add(v);}if(out.size()!=n)out.clear();}
    for(int x:out)if(x>=k)return x;return -1;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

links is a square 0/1 directed adjacency matrix. Queue all zero-indegree vertices in increasing order; pop FIFO, visit neighbors in increasing order, and enqueue a neighbor when its indegree becomes zero. Produce this vertex order, or no values if a cycle prevents a complete order.

Return the first value in the derived sequence that is at least k, or -1 if there is none. Return the value, not its index.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(V^2); space O(n).

